Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-12-2001||03:45 PM||||(Huge chevy Beauville van btw). Ok, the driver side isn't so bad.. You bring the window down, and then when you want to bring it up again, it will go 3/4-4/5 of the way, and then stop. then you can wait about 20 seconds, and it will go up another inch, and then so on. (If you wait a long while, it will go up pretty far). Overheating or something? Now the passenger side. Won't go down or up without help. Actually, now it's jammed. I used to be able to raise it at night by pressing on it and pushing up as hard as I could (or grabbing and pulling) but now, no cigar. Adjustment? Motor? Help me please.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||roadwarrior||08-12-2001||05:45 PM||||check all your electrical connections and also try lifting your switch covers and cleaning you switch contacts||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||08-13-2001||02:18 AM||||Nothing to do with that. The window sort of tries to move (course, it only makes it about .5 mm)||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||08-13-2001||02:20 AM||||What year is it?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||08-13-2001||11:27 AM||||I talked to these guys about the same thing happening to the window motors on my 1989 Bonneville.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.windowmotorworld.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.windowmotorworld.com/</A> <P>They said the motors aren't worth salvaging since they are riveted together and it would take a jig to get them back together correctly.  New motors are $59.95 US each.<P>He said that there is a thermal switch which wears out gradually.  It has a carbon element that gets weak.  This causes a lot of electrical resistance which results in slow to no operation.<P>The new motors come with hardware to install them without needing the large rivet gun.  He also said the new motors will be stronger than the OEM ones were when new.||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||08-13-2001||01:56 PM||||It's a 91 I believe.. Is there any way to test them to make sure it is the motor? I would hate to cut out the whole side of the door to find out thats not the problem..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> But doesn't it seem pretty strange that it's basically jammed? Shouldn't I be able to pull it up?||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||08-13-2001||02:26 PM||||No Johny, that's normal for a window motor that's going. I just changed my 94 Camaro's window motor and it was doing exactly the same thing. I got one at a junkyard and ops checked it before I installed it and found it to be defective. So I went to Autozone and got one for $70, installed it, and it works great. I would suggest that. Oh, and the job sucks. I don't know how that one is installed, but my Camaro has plastic (ABS?) doors that I had to drill through to get to a couple of the rivets, and then enlarge to get the nose of the gun in there. But honestly, even if the car is apiece of crap, don't buy a use one, you time is worth more than that. Good luck.<P>Oh, and as far as it being jammed, the motor probably has a brake on it, but what you feel is the resistance from the motor. This sounds like classic window regulator (motor) failure.<p>[This message has been edited by EdsB52 (edited 08-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||08-13-2001||04:31 PM||||Hmmm... See, I'm going to look into that. It just feels really really jammed. Like me and my friend can't pull it up (while pressing up on the switch). That normal?||||reg||1
Z||000008||falconhulk||08-14-2001||03:15 PM||||I have the same problem with my 87 firebird.  Driversa side window cant be pulled up or down.  I measured the resistance from the motor term.'s to ground and found that one of them had a 1k ohm resistance.....I pulled the door cover and could hear the motor trying to turn but it was to weak.  I measured the resistance of the good motor and there it was infinite.  This 1k resistance was a semi short in the motorto the motor housing.  Since the motor housing is grounded it was like a short to ground.  The window in the firebird is very heavy, not sure about the fiero.  I needed another person to hold the window as I removed the motor.  I dont know why they could not have used something other then rivets toconnect thses to the door frame!!  Pain in the ass||||reg||1
Z||000009||72vinman||08-14-2001||03:44 PM||||I had the same problem with my 92 Grand Am Gt. It continued to get worse and worse until it stopped working. Replaced it with a used one from the used parts store and it worked great.<BR><P>------------------<BR>72vinman@home.com<BR>Black 86GT||||reg||7
Z||000010||EdsB52||08-14-2001||04:08 PM||||Sorry Johnny, I was in denial also! That motor is a pain in the ass to change and it's so easy to hope it's a switch or bad connection, but unfortunately it's most likely the motor(s) bro.  <P>Just have lots of patience and set aside a weekend. Have your drill motors and bits on hand. Carefully remove the inner door panels and go to work. Once you get done, you'll be happy. I put mine back in with rivets, but bolts will also work. I think rivets are better because they expand in the hole and take up any lash that might be present when using bolts, considering the holes are non-precision. If you use bolts and any lash exists, the window might make a clunk at either end of the window travel. Good luck, my friend.<P>As far as the resistance, I didn't mean electrical resistance, I meant mechanical resistance. There might be a brake inside the motor that prohibits the gear from turning unless electricity is applied. Either way, it's normal for the window to not travel, even with force applied, with no electricity. Don't force too much or you'll bend some parts relevant to the window mechanism.||||reg||1
